DSP.mod(15,{"m15-resume":{"id":"m15-resume","slug":"resume-checklist","m":15,"title":"Resume checklist","min":18,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":[],"tags":["resume","careers"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"How to build a one-page resume for 0 to 2 years of experience: structure, skills, bullets, ATS-friendliness and honesty, with before-and-after examples.","keyTerms":["one-page resume","ATS","keywords","quantified bullets","skills grouping","projects section"],"interviewQs":["Walk me through your resume.","Which skills on your resume can I grill you on?"],"mistakes":["Listing skills you cannot defend","Two pages of tools with no outcomes","Fancy formatting that breaks parsers"],"practice":"Rewrite your resume to one page using the checklist, then have a friend mark every line they would ask about.","related":["m14-resume-bullets","m15-tell-me"],"points":"- Header with email, phone, LinkedIn, GitHub, portfolio\n- 2-line summary (optional), tailored to the role\n- Skills grouped: Languages (Python, SQL), Libraries (pandas, scikit-learn, PyTorch), Tools (Git, Docker, Tableau, Airflow), Cloud (AWS/Azure/GCP)\n- Experience/internships with quantified impact bullets\n- 3-4 projects with links and one-line results\n- Education, relevant certifications (only meaningful ones), Kaggle rank, publications, hackathons\n- ATS-friendly: simple formatting, keywords from the JD, PDF, no photos/tables/graphics\n- Every skill listed is one you can be grilled on"},"intuitive":"<p>A resume is a one-page menu for the interviewer: they will order from it, so only list what you can cook well. Put your contact details and links at the top, a short tailored summary if you like, your skills grouped clearly, your best experience and projects with results, and your education. Keep the format plain so the computer that scans resumes (the ATS) can read it, and use words from the job description where they are honestly true. Every skill you list is an invitation to be grilled on it.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> The resume decides who gets an interview, then steers the questions you will face.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> One page, results-focused, honest, plain formatting, tailored keywords.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;A data science resume is one page for early career, scannable in thirty seconds. At the top: contact and links to GitHub or portfolio. Then a short skills section grouped by area, projects or experience as impact-driven bullets with numbers, and education. Keep layout plain, one font, no photos or tables that applicant tracking systems cannot read. Tailor keywords to the job description honestly. Every line should be something I can discuss for five minutes. Proofread for typos. Save as a PDF named with my name. The catch: the resume&#39;s job is to earn the interview, so I put the most relevant and strongest items on the first half of the page.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"The 30-second scan test","html":"<p>A recruiter gives your resume about 30 seconds and reads mostly the top third. Check what that top third says.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Cover everything below the top third</strong> and read only what is visible.</li>\n<li><strong>Does it say who you are and what you want?</strong> A one-line headline such as &quot;Data analyst moving into data science; SQL, Python, experimentation&quot; beats a paragraph of adjectives.</li>\n<li><strong>Is your strongest evidence there?</strong> The best project or job bullet, with a number, should be visible in that first glance, not on line 25.</li>\n<li><strong>Is it machine-readable?</strong> A single column, standard headings (Experience, Projects, Skills, Education), no tables or text inside images. Applicant tracking systems read text, not layout.</li>\n<li><strong>Cut anything you cannot defend:</strong> tools you used once, vague phrases such as &quot;hard-working team player&quot;, and generic coursework.</li>\n<li><strong>Repeat the test with a friend:</strong> give them 30 seconds, take the resume away, and ask what they remember. If it is not your top skill and your best result, reorder.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A resume is an advertisement for an interview, read in seconds. Put the strongest evidence where the eye lands first.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-checklist-with-reasons","text":"The checklist with reasons","level":3},{"id":"h-before-and-after","text":"Before and after","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"cl","id":"m15-resume.c1","kind":"list","items":["Header with email, phone, LinkedIn, GitHub, portfolio","2-line summary (optional), tailored to the role","Skills grouped: Languages (Python, SQL), Libraries (pandas, scikit-learn, PyTorch), Tools (Git, Docker, Tableau, Airflow), Cloud (AWS/Azure/GCP)","Experience/internships with quantified impact bullets","3-4 projects with links and one-line results","Education, relevant certifications (only meaningful ones), Kaggle rank, publications, hackathons","ATS-friendly: simple formatting, keywords from the JD, PDF, no photos/tables/graphics","Every skill listed is one you can be grilled on"]},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-checklist-with-reasons\">The checklist with reasons</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <strong>Header</strong> with email, phone, LinkedIn, GitHub and portfolio links (recruiters click them)</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <strong>2-line summary</strong> (optional), tailored to the role (what you do and your strongest angle)</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <strong>Skills grouped:</strong> Languages (Python, SQL), Libraries (pandas, scikit-learn, PyTorch), Tools (Git, Docker, Tableau, Airflow), Cloud (AWS/Azure/GCP): groups are scannable</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <strong>Experience or internships</strong> with <strong>quantified impact</strong> bullets</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <strong>3 to 4 projects</strong> with links and one-line results</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <strong>Education</strong>, only meaningful certifications, Kaggle rank, publications, hackathons</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <strong>ATS-friendly:</strong> simple formatting, keywords from the job description, exported as <strong>PDF</strong>, no photos, tables or graphics</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <strong>Every skill is one you can be grilled on</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-before-and-after\">Before and after</h3>\n<p><strong>Skills line.</strong> Before: &quot;Python, SQL, ML, DL, NLP, Computer Vision, Big Data, Spark, Hadoop, TensorFlow, PyTorch, Keras, AWS, GCP, Azure, Docker, Kubernetes.&quot; After: &quot;Languages: Python, SQL. Data/ML: pandas, scikit-learn, LightGBM, PyTorch (basic). Tools: Git, Docker, Power BI. Cloud: AWS (S3, Athena).&quot; A short list you can defend beats a long one you cannot.</p>\n<p><strong>Project bullet.</strong> Before: &quot;Predicted loan defaults using machine learning.&quot; After: &quot;Built a loan-default scorecard (logistic regression, WoE/IV) on 30k applications; KS 0.41, Gini 0.58 on a time-based hold-out; documented each variable&#39;s effect for credit-policy review.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Experience bullet (intern).</strong> Before: &quot;Assisted the analytics team with reports.&quot; After: &quot;Automated the weekly sales report in SQL and Python (replacing 3 hours of manual Excel work) and flagged a 7% regional reporting discrepancy to the finance team.&quot;</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<aside class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Tailor, do not lie</div><div class=\"callout-b\"><p>Reorder and emphasise real experience to match the role: for a product company, lead with SQL, experimentation and metrics; for an AI startup, lead with LLM/RAG and deployment projects. Never add a skill you cannot discuss.</p>\n</div></aside>"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m15-resume~1","q":"Your resume lists &quot;Spark&quot; but you have only run a tutorial. The interviewer asks about it. What do you say?","a":"<p>Be honest about depth: &quot;I completed a PySpark tutorial and used it to process a 5 GB dataset in a personal project; I understand lazy evaluation, shuffles and broadcast joins conceptually but have not run production jobs.&quot; Then show the conceptual knowledge. Better still, remove or qualify it (&quot;Spark (basic)&quot;) before the interview.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m15-resume~ca0_1","q":"Why use a plain layout?","a":"<p>Applicant tracking systems parse text; tables, columns and graphics can scramble it, and humans scan simple layouts faster.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m15-resume~ca0_2","q":"How do you tailor a resume to a job description?","a":"<p>Mirror honest keywords, reorder bullets so the most relevant projects come first, and highlight matching skills.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m15-resume~ca0_3","q":"How long should an early-career resume be?","a":"<p>One page.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m15-resume~ta1_1","q":"<p>A fresher&#39;s resume opens with an objective (&quot;Seeking a challenging role in a reputed organisation...&quot;), then education, then a skills list of 20 tools, and puts projects on page 2. What three changes would you make?</p>\n","a":"<p>(1) Replace the objective with a one-line headline naming the target role and two or three core skills. (2) Move the two strongest projects, with outcome numbers, directly under the headline, above education, keeping the page to one. (3) Cut the skills list to the tools actually used in those projects, grouped by type, so every listed tool has evidence behind it.</p>\n"}]},"m15-tell-me":{"id":"m15-tell-me","slug":"tell-me-about-yourself","m":15,"title":"\"Tell me about yourself\"","min":15,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":[],"tags":["behavioral","communication"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"A structure for \"Tell me about yourself\", three worked examples (fresher, experienced, career switcher) and how to tailor it.","keyTerms":["present past future","60 to 90 seconds","key project with result","why this company"],"interviewQs":["Tell me about yourself.","Why this role and company?"],"mistakes":["Reciting the resume chronologically","Going over two minutes","Ending without a link to the role"],"practice":"Write and record a 75-second answer; listen once for clarity and once for energy, then trim.","related":["m15-behavioral","m14-resume-bullets"],"points":"Structure: Present (who you are, current focus) -> Past (education/experience highlights, key project with result) -> Future (why this role/company).\n\n> \"I'm a data science graduate with a year of experience at [X], where I built a demand-forecasting model that cut stock-outs by 12%. I'm strongest in SQL, Python, and classical ML, and I enjoy turning messy data into decisions. Recently I built a RAG assistant over policy documents and deployed it with FastAPI. I'm excited about [Company] because of [specific data problem], and I'd like to contribute to [team area] while growing in [skill].\""},"intuitive":"<p>&quot;Tell me about yourself&quot; is not a request for your life story; it is your 90-second trailer. Say who you are now (your present focus), how you got here (one or two highlights with a result), and why you are excited about this role (the future). Practise it until it is smooth but not robotic, and change the last sentence for every company.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> It is the first question in almost every interview and sets the tone.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Present, past, future: sixty to ninety seconds, tailored ending.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;&#39;Tell me about yourself&#39; is a ninety-second pitch. I use present, past, future. Present: who I am now, my role or studies and my strongest relevant skill. Past: two or three experiences or projects that built that skill, with one concrete result. Future: why this role at this company fits my direction. I tailor it to the job and do not recite my whole resume or personal history. I end with something that invites a follow-up, like a project I am proud of. I rehearse it aloud until it sounds natural, not memorised. The catch: it is the first impression and sets the questions I will get, so I plant hooks for topics I want to discuss.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Write a 90-second introduction","html":"<p>About 200 words, spoken. Present, then past, then future.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Present (about 20 seconds):</strong> &quot;I&#39;m a data analyst at a logistics company, where I build SQL reporting and forecasting for the operations team. My strongest skill is turning messy operational data into decisions.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Past, the evidence (about 45 seconds):</strong> &quot;Over the last two years I automated our weekly delivery reports, cutting a day of manual work to minutes, and I built a demand forecast that reduced rider shortages at peak hours by about 15%. To go deeper into modelling, I completed a churn project end to end: features as of a prediction date, a time-based split and a cost-based threshold.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Future, why this role (about 20 seconds):</strong> &quot;I&#39;m looking for a data science role where forecasting and experimentation drive the product, which is why your team&#39;s work on delivery-time prediction interests me.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Stop.</strong> No life story, no hobbies unless asked.</li>\n<li><strong>Plant hooks:</strong> the forecast and the churn project are the two things you <em>want</em> to be asked about. You have just chosen the next question.</li>\n<li><strong>Rehearse aloud with a timer</strong> until it lands between 75 and 100 seconds and sounds conversational.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The introduction sets the agenda. Present, past and future, with one concrete result, steer the interview towards your best material.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-structure","text":"The structure","level":3},{"id":"h-three-examples","text":"Three examples","level":3},{"id":"h-tailoring","text":"Tailoring","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p>Structure: <strong>Present</strong> (who you are, current focus) -&gt; <strong>Past</strong> (education/experience highlights, key project with result) -&gt; <strong>Future</strong> (why this role/company).</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>&quot;I&#39;m a data science graduate with a year of experience at [X], where I built a demand-forecasting model that cut stock-outs by 12%. I&#39;m strongest in SQL, Python, and classical ML, and I enjoy turning messy data into decisions. Recently I built a RAG assistant over policy documents and deployed it with FastAPI. I&#39;m excited about [Company] because of [specific data problem], and I&#39;d like to contribute to [team area] while growing in [skill].&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-structure\">The structure</h3>\n<p><strong>Present</strong> (who you are and your current focus) → <strong>Past</strong> (education or experience highlights with one key project and a result) → <strong>Future</strong> (why this role and company, what you want to contribute and learn).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-three-examples\">Three examples</h3>\n<p><strong>Fresher.</strong> &quot;I&#39;m a final-year statistics student who has spent the last year focusing on data science, mostly SQL, Python and classical machine learning. During an internship at a retail company I built a demand-forecasting prototype that cut forecast error by 12% against their spreadsheet method. Most recently I built and deployed a RAG assistant over insurance policy documents. I&#39;m excited about Flipkart because of the scale of its supply-chain forecasting problems, and I&#39;d like to contribute to the demand team while deepening my experimentation skills.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>One to two years of experience.</strong> &quot;I&#39;m a data analyst with about 18 months in an e-commerce company, where I own the weekly retention analysis and built a churn model used by the CRM team, which improved offer targeting by about 2.5x versus random. I&#39;m strongest in SQL and in translating messy questions into analyses. I&#39;m now looking to move into a data scientist role where I can work on experimentation and modelling end to end, which is why the experimentation platform work at your company appeals to me.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Career switcher (software engineer).</strong> &quot;I&#39;ve spent two years as a backend engineer, where I got comfortable with production systems, APIs and testing. I kept gravitating to the data side, so I completed a structured data science course and built three projects: a credit-risk scorecard, a demand forecast, and a FastAPI-deployed recommender. My engineering background means I think about reproducibility and deployment from day one. I&#39;d like to bring that to an ML team working on user-facing products.&quot;</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-tailoring\">Tailoring</h3>\n<p>Replace the last sentence with a specific data problem at that company (from their product, blog or job description). Keep spoken length to 60 to 90 seconds (about 150 to 200 words).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m15-tell-me~1","q":"What do you do if the interviewer follows up with &quot;Why are you changing roles?&quot;","a":"<p>Give a positive, forward-looking answer: what you want more of (modelling, experimentation, ownership) rather than what you dislike. &quot;I&#39;ve learned a lot in analytics and I want to work on the prediction and experimentation side more deeply; this role gives me that.&quot; Do not criticise your current employer.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m15-tell-me~ca0_1","q":"What are the three parts of the answer?","a":"<p>Present (who I am and my core strength), past (relevant experience with a result), future (why this role).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m15-tell-me~ca0_2","q":"How long should it be?","a":"<p>About 60 to 90 seconds.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m15-tell-me~ca0_3","q":"Why mention one concrete project result?","a":"<p>It makes the pitch credible and gives the interviewer an easy, favourable follow-up question.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m15-tell-me~ta1_1","q":"<p>Write only the &quot;future&quot; sentence for a candidate applying to a fintech company&#39;s risk analytics team, who has built a credit-default project.</p>\n","a":"<p>For example: &quot;I want to work where models directly shape lending decisions and have to be explainable to risk and compliance teams, which is what drew me to your risk analytics team; my credit-default project, where I built a scorecard with reason codes, is the closest I have come so far, and I&#39;d like to do it with real portfolio data.&quot; Specific to the team, linked to evidence, and forward-looking.</p>\n"}]},"m15-behavioral":{"id":"m15-behavioral","slug":"behavioural-questions-and-star-stories","m":15,"title":"Behavioural questions and STAR stories","min":30,"type":"practice","priority":"High","prereq":[],"tags":["behavioral","STAR"],"qa":14,"ai":{"scope":"The STAR method, how to build a bank of five or six adaptable stories, and model guidance for the nine common behavioural themes, with a fully worked example.","keyTerms":["STAR method","ownership","failure","conflict","ambiguity","influence","deadline","feedback","impact","story bank"],"interviewQs":["Tell me about a time you failed.","Describe a disagreement with a teammate or stakeholder.","Tell me about a project you drove end to end."],"mistakes":["Describing the team's work instead of your own actions","Stories with no result or learning","Reusing one story for everything without adapting"],"practice":"Write a STAR outline for each of your six stories, then map each story to at least three themes.","related":["m15-tell-me","m14-deep-dive","m15-hr"],"points":"STAR: Situation, Task, Action, Result (plus learning).\n\nQ: Tell me about a project you drove end to end.\nA: \n\nQ: A time your model/analysis was wrong or a project failed.\nA: \n\nQ: Disagreement with a teammate/stakeholder.\nA: \n\nQ: A vague problem statement you had to structure.\nA: \n\nQ: Something technical you learned quickly and applied.\nA: \n\nQ: Convincing a non-technical stakeholder with data.\nA: \n\nQ: Tight deadline with trade-offs.\nA: \n\nQ: Critical feedback you received and acted on.\nA: \n\nQ: Your biggest measurable contribution.\nA: \n\nPrepare 5-6 stories that can be adapted to many questions."},"intuitive":"<p>Behavioural questions (&quot;tell me about a time when...&quot;) are checking how you actually behave at work. Use STAR: explain the <strong>S</strong>ituation, your <strong>T</strong>ask, the <strong>A</strong>ctions <em>you</em> took (the longest part) and the <strong>R</strong>esult, plus what you learned. Prepare five or six real stories in advance; each story can answer several different questions if you tilt it slightly. Keep each to about two minutes.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Hiring managers and GCCs (Amazon-style leadership principles) weight these rounds heavily.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Five or six real stories, told as Situation, Task, Action, Result, learning.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Behavioural questions ask for past examples. I answer with STAR: Situation, Task, Action, Result. Briefly set the context, say what my responsibility was, describe what I specifically did, the &#39;I&#39; not the &#39;we&#39;, and end with the result, quantified if possible, and what I learned. I keep five to six prepared stories that cover conflict, failure, tight deadlines, leadership, ambiguity and influencing without authority, and reuse them flexibly. Most of the time should be on Action and Result. For failure questions, choose a real, modest failure and show the learning. The catch: a story with no clear result, or one that blames others, fails; I end every story with the outcome.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Turn raw notes into a STAR story","html":"<p>Raw notes: <em>&quot;Dashboard numbers did not match finance. Big argument. I found the issue. Fixed. Everyone happy.&quot;</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Situation (1 or 2 sentences):</strong> &quot;Our weekly revenue dashboard showed 8% more revenue than finance&#39;s report, and leadership had started to distrust both.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Task (1 sentence):</strong> &quot;As the analyst who owned the dashboard, I had to find the cause before the quarterly review in a week.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Action (the core, said with &quot;I&quot;):</strong> &quot;I compared the two at order level for one week and found two differences: finance excluded cancelled orders and recorded refunds on the refund date, while the dashboard used the order date. I documented both definitions, met the finance lead to agree one definition per metric, changed the dashboard query, and added a reconciliation check that flags any gap over 1%.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Result (a number and a lesson):</strong> &quot;The two reports matched within 0.2% from the following week, and the reconciliation check has caught two pipeline issues since. I now agree metric definitions in writing before I build any report.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Timing:</strong> about 10% situation, 10% task, 60% action, 20% result. Around 90 seconds.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> STAR turns &quot;I fixed it&quot; into evidence: what you did, how you decided, what changed, and what you learned.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-star-in-detail","text":"STAR in detail","level":3},{"id":"h-a-fully-worked-story-ownership-ambiguity-impact","text":"A fully worked story (ownership, ambiguity, impact)","level":3},{"id":"h-model-guidance-for-the-nine-themes","text":"Model guidance for the nine themes","level":3},{"id":"h-building-your-story-bank","text":"Building your story bank","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>STAR:</strong> Situation, Task, Action, Result (plus learning).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"15.3#1","src":"c","q":"Tell me about a project you drove end to end.","a":"<p><strong>Ownership.</strong> Choose a project where you drove the scope, not just the code. Cover: how you framed the problem with stakeholders, key decisions and trade-offs (metric, data, model), obstacles you unblocked, how you measured impact, and what you would change. Make the <strong>result</strong> concrete (a metric moved, a decision changed).</p>\n","s":"Use STAR: the problem and stakes, your role, the actions you personally took, and a quantified result and lesson.","label":"Ownership"},{"k":"qa","id":"15.3#2","src":"c","q":"A time your model/analysis was wrong or a project failed.","a":"<p><strong>Failure.</strong> Pick a real but not catastrophic mistake. Explain what went wrong (your role in it, not blame), how you noticed, what you did to contain and fix it, and, most importantly, the <strong>specific process change</strong> you made afterwards (for example adding a leakage check, a data validation step or a code review). Interviewers assess accountability and learning.</p>\n","s":"Own a real failure, explain the cause, how you fixed it, and the process change you made since.","m":"<p><strong>Structure.</strong> Situation (one sentence), what went wrong and your part in it, how you detected it, the fix, and the new habit: for example adding a data-quality check or reviewing assumptions with a colleague.</p>\n","label":"Failure"},{"k":"qa","id":"15.3#3","src":"c","q":"Disagreement with a teammate/stakeholder.","a":"<p><strong>Conflict.</strong> Show respectful, data-driven handling: what the disagreement was about, how you sought to understand the other view, what evidence or experiment you proposed, how you reached a decision (even if you were overruled) and what the relationship looked like afterwards. Avoid villains and &quot;I was right&quot; endings.</p>\n","s":"Show you listened, used data to resolve it, kept the relationship, and reached an outcome both sides could accept.","label":"Conflict"},{"k":"qa","id":"15.3#4","src":"c","q":"A vague problem statement you had to structure.","a":"<p><strong>Ambiguity.</strong> Describe a vague request (&quot;improve engagement&quot;) and how you structured it: clarifying questions, defining metrics, breaking it into testable pieces, prioritising by impact and effort, aligning with stakeholders on scope, and delivering an iterative result. The hallmark is turning fog into a plan.</p>\n","s":"Describe the clarifying questions, how you defined the problem, the metric and scope, and what you delivered.","label":"Ambiguity"},{"k":"qa","id":"15.3#5","src":"c","q":"Something technical you learned quickly and applied.","a":"<p><strong>Learning.</strong> Show a fast, deliberate learning loop: what you needed to learn and why, how you learned it (docs, a small prototype, a mentor), how you applied it within a deadline, and the outcome (for example picking up PySpark in two weeks to process a 50 GB file). Emphasise application, not just study.</p>\n","s":"Name the skill, how you learned it fast (docs, practice, mentors), and the real result you applied it to.","label":"Learning"},{"k":"qa","id":"15.3#6","src":"c","q":"Convincing a non-technical stakeholder with data.","a":"<p><strong>Influence.</strong> Explain how you tailored the message to a non-technical stakeholder: led with the answer and impact, used one clear chart, addressed their concerns and risks, and gave options. State the outcome (they approved, changed a decision). Mention how you handled pushback.</p>\n","s":"Start with their goal, show one clear chart and a recommendation in plain language, address the risks, and describe the decision it enabled.","m":"<p><strong>Example.</strong> &quot;I had to convince marketing to stop a discount. I showed one chart: customers who got the discount did not buy more than a matched group, and it cost 8% margin. They agreed to a smaller test.&quot;</p>\n","label":"Influence"},{"k":"qa","id":"15.3#7","src":"c","q":"Tight deadline with trade-offs.","a":"<p><strong>Deadline.</strong> Show prioritisation under pressure: what was essential vs nice-to-have, the trade-offs you made and communicated (for example a simpler model with a documented limitation), how you protected quality where it mattered (validation, no leakage) and what you delivered on time. Mention the follow-up to pay down shortcuts.</p>\n","s":"Say what you prioritised and why, what you cut or deferred, how you communicated, and the outcome.","label":"Deadline"},{"k":"qa","id":"15.3#8","src":"c","q":"Critical feedback you received and acted on.","a":"<p><strong>Feedback.</strong> Pick genuinely critical feedback (for example unclear communication or underdocumented code), show you listened without defensiveness, asked for examples, made a concrete change (a README template, summary-first emails) and the improvement that followed, ideally confirmed by the person who gave it.</p>\n","s":"Quote the feedback, explain what you changed, and show the measurable improvement afterwards.","label":"Feedback"},{"k":"qa","id":"15.3#9","src":"c","q":"Your biggest measurable contribution.","a":"<p><strong>Impact.</strong> Choose the contribution with the clearest, most defensible number (revenue, hours saved, error reduced) and explain <strong>your</strong> part versus the team&#39;s, how the number was measured, and why it mattered to the business. Be honest about attribution.</p>\n","s":"Pick one concrete project with a number you can defend, and say what you did to cause it.","m":"<p><strong>Choose carefully.</strong> Prefer a result with a measure (time saved, revenue, accuracy over baseline) and a clear link between your action and the outcome. Be ready for &quot;how did you measure it?&quot;.</p>\n","label":"Impact"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p>Prepare <strong>5-6 stories</strong> that can be adapted to many questions.</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-star-in-detail\">STAR in detail</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Situation:</strong> one or two sentences of context.</li>\n<li><strong>Task:</strong> what you were responsible for or trying to achieve.</li>\n<li><strong>Action:</strong> the specific steps <strong>you</strong> took (use &quot;I&quot;), including decisions and trade-offs. This is the longest part.</li>\n<li><strong>Result:</strong> the outcome, quantified if possible, plus what you learned.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-a-fully-worked-story-ownership-ambiguity-impact\">A fully worked story (ownership, ambiguity, impact)</h3>\n<p><strong>Situation:</strong> &quot;At my company the marketing team said repeat purchase rates were falling but had no idea why. <strong>Task:</strong> I was asked to find out and recommend what to do. <strong>Action:</strong> I first defined &#39;repeat purchase&#39; precisely with the stakeholders, then decomposed it by cohort, channel and delivery experience in SQL. I found that customers whose first order arrived more than 15 minutes late had a 30% lower repeat rate, concentrated in two zones. I validated it by comparing similar customers across on-time and late deliveries and presented it with one chart. I proposed a small compensation credit for late first orders and worked with operations on those zones. <strong>Result:</strong> we ran the credit as an A/B test; repeat rate in the treated group rose by 4 points, and the approach was adopted in five more cities. <strong>Learning:</strong> define the metric first, and pair the analysis with an actionable recommendation.&quot;</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-model-guidance-for-the-nine-themes\">Model guidance for the nine themes</h3>\n<h3 id=\"h-building-your-story-bank\">Building your story bank</h3>\n<p>Write one STAR outline per story: a project you drove, a failure, a conflict, a tight deadline, a moment you influenced with data, and something you learned fast. Tag each with the themes it can answer, and keep each to two minutes when spoken.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m15-behavioral~1","q":"The interviewer says &quot;Tell me about a time you disagreed with your manager.&quot; You only have a mild example. Is that a problem?","a":"<p>No: a mild, real example told well is better than a dramatic invented one. Show how you raised your view with evidence (for example a quick analysis), listened to their constraints, and how you committed to the decision (or how it was resolved). Interviewers look for professionalism and collaboration, not drama.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m15-behavioral~ca0_1","q":"What does STAR stand for, and where should most of the time go?","a":"<p>Situation, Task, Action, Result; spend most time on Action and Result.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m15-behavioral~ca0_2","q":"&quot;Tell me about a failure.&quot; What makes a good answer?","a":"<p>A real, owned failure with limited stakes, what you did to fix it, and a concrete change in how you work since.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m15-behavioral~ca0_3","q":"How many stories should you prepare?","a":"<p>Five or six flexible ones that can be adapted to different questions.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m15-behavioral~ta2_1","q":"<p>Rewrite this action sentence so the interviewer can assess <em>you</em>: &quot;We cleaned the data, built a model and presented it to the client.&quot;</p>\n","a":"<p>For example: &quot;I wrote the data validation checks that removed 4% duplicate records, I built and compared the baseline and gradient-boosting models, and I prepared and delivered the five-slide summary of the results to the client&#39;s operations head; my teammate handled the data extraction.&quot; Specific actions with &quot;I&quot;, and honest credit for the parts others did.</p>\n"}]},"m15-hr":{"id":"m15-hr","slug":"hr-and-logistics-questions-including-salary","m":15,"title":"HR and logistics questions, including salary","min":25,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":[],"tags":["hr","salary","negotiation"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Common HR and logistics questions with model answers, how to research and discuss compensation in India, and good questions to ask the interviewer.","keyTerms":["why data science","strengths and weaknesses","leaving your job","notice period","CTC","expected CTC","ESOPs","questions to ask"],"interviewQs":["What is your expected CTC?","What is your weakness?","Why are you leaving your current job?","Do you have questions for us?"],"mistakes":["Naming a number before researching the range","Badmouthing the current employer","Having no questions for the interviewer"],"practice":"Write your answers to \"why data science?\" and \"why this company?\" and research the salary range for one target role on two sites.","related":["m15-tell-me","m15-interview-day"],"points":"- Why data science? Why this company/role? Where do you see yourself in 3-5 years?\n- Strengths and weaknesses (real weakness + mitigation).\n- Why are you leaving your current job (positive framing, no badmouthing)?\n- Notice period, current CTC, expected CTC: research ranges on AmbitionBox, Glassdoor, Levels.fyi, LinkedIn; give a researched range, emphasize flexibility based on total package and growth. Know fixed vs variable, joining bonus, ESOPs.\n- Relocation, work mode (remote/hybrid), bond/service agreements (read carefully).\n- Questions to ask them: What does success look like in 6 months? What does the data stack/team structure look like? How are models deployed and monitored? How do data scientists work with product/engineering? What are current biggest data challenges?"},"intuitive":"<p>The HR round checks that you will be a good, reliable colleague and that the practical details (salary, notice period, location) line up. Answer honestly and positively: show real motivation for data science and for this company, name a real weakness you are actively improving, and talk about why you are moving <em>towards</em> something, not running away. For salary, do your research, give a range, and focus on the total package and growth, not one number.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Offers can be lost or left unnegotiated here.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Be prepared, be honest, be positive, and research before you talk numbers.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;HR and logistics rounds cover motivation, notice period, current and expected compensation, location and timeline. I prepare honest, concise answers. &#39;Why this company?&#39; needs specific research on its product and data challenges. For salary, I research the market range first and I try to let them name a range first; if pushed, I give a researched range, with my target in the lower half, based on role and market rather than my current pay, where permitted. I also consider total compensation: bonus, equity, growth and learning. I am polite and flexible but know my floor. The catch: negotiating happens after an offer, so I stay positive and collaborative and never lie about other offers or my current pay.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Compare two offers by total value, not by the headline","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Offer A</th><th>Offer B</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fixed pay</td><td>14.0 lakh</td><td>12.5 lakh</td></tr><tr><td>Variable (target)</td><td>10% of fixed</td><td>20% of fixed</td></tr><tr><td>Joining bonus</td><td>none</td><td>1.0 lakh (one-time)</td></tr><tr><td>Stock (ESOPs)</td><td>none</td><td>2.0 lakh a year, vesting over 4 years</td></tr><tr><td>Learning</td><td>small team, no mentor</td><td>senior data scientists, structured reviews</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Expected cash in year 1.</strong> A: 14.0 + 1.4 (if the variable is paid in full) = 15.4 lakh. B: 12.5 + 2.5 + 1.0 = 16.0 lakh.</li>\n<li><strong>Discount what is uncertain.</strong> If variable pay usually comes in at about 70% of target: A becomes 14.0 + 0.98 = 14.98; B becomes 12.5 + 1.75 + 1.0 = 15.25.</li>\n<li><strong>Year 2 onwards</strong> (no joining bonus): A ≈ 14.98, B ≈ 14.25 in cash, plus B&#39;s stock, whose value depends on the company and on liquidity. Treat it as a possible upside, not as salary.</li>\n<li><strong>Add what does not fit in a table:</strong> B offers mentorship and harder problems, which compound into your next salary.</li>\n<li><strong>Negotiate with data:</strong> &quot;I&#39;m excited about B. Given A&#39;s higher fixed pay, could the fixed component move to 13.5 lakh?&quot; Calm, specific and honest about the existence of the other offer.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Compare offers on expected, risk-adjusted value over two or three years, plus learning, not on the headline number.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-model-answers","text":"Model answers","level":3},{"id":"h-compensation-in-india","text":"Compensation in India","level":3},{"id":"h-questions-to-ask-them-ask-two-or-three","text":"Questions to ask them (ask two or three)","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li>Why data science? Why this company/role? Where do you see yourself in 3-5 years?</li>\n<li>Strengths and weaknesses (real weakness + mitigation).</li>\n<li>Why are you leaving your current job (positive framing, no badmouthing)?</li>\n<li><strong>Notice period, current CTC, expected CTC:</strong> research ranges on AmbitionBox, Glassdoor, Levels.fyi, LinkedIn; give a researched range, emphasize flexibility based on total package and growth. Know fixed vs variable, joining bonus, ESOPs.</li>\n<li>Relocation, work mode (remote/hybrid), bond/service agreements (read carefully).</li>\n<li><strong>Questions to ask them:</strong> What does success look like in 6 months? What does the data stack/team structure look like? How are models deployed and monitored? How do data scientists work with product/engineering? What are current biggest data challenges?</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-model-answers\">Model answers</h3>\n<p><strong>Why data science? Why this company or role? Where in 3 to 5 years?</strong> Connect your motivation to a concrete experience (&quot;I enjoy turning messy data into decisions; my churn project showed me how an analysis changes what a team does&quot;), name something specific about the company&#39;s data problems, and describe growth (deeper ML ownership, leading a small project) without sounding entitled.</p>\n<p><strong>Strengths and weaknesses.</strong> Strengths: two or three supported by evidence. Weakness: a <strong>real</strong> one, not a disguised strength, plus the <strong>mitigation</strong>: &quot;I tend to over-engineer early versions; I now set a time-box and ship a simple baseline first.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Why are you leaving?</strong> Positive framing, no badmouthing: &quot;I&#39;ve learned a lot here, and I&#39;m looking for more ownership of modelling and experimentation than my current role offers.&quot;</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-compensation-in-india\">Compensation in India</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>CTC</strong> (cost to company)</td><td>total annual cost to the employer: fixed + variable + benefits</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fixed</strong></td><td>guaranteed pay (basic + allowances); counts for in-hand and loans</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Variable / bonus</strong></td><td>performance-linked; often not guaranteed in full</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Joining bonus, relocation</strong></td><td>one-off; often with a clawback if you leave early</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ESOPs / RSUs</strong></td><td>equity; understand vesting schedule, strike price and liquidity</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Notice period</strong></td><td>commonly 30 to 90 days; ask about buyout options</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Research</strong> ranges on AmbitionBox, Glassdoor, Levels.fyi and LinkedIn, and ask recruiters for the band. <strong>Script for expected CTC:</strong> &quot;Based on my research for this role and my skills, I&#39;m looking at ₹X to ₹Y, but I&#39;m flexible depending on the overall package and growth opportunities. Could you share the band for this role?&quot; Emphasise <strong>total package</strong> (fixed vs variable, ESOPs, learning), not only the headline. Do not reveal a number before you know the band if you can avoid it; be honest about your current CTC if asked directly.</p>\n<p>Also know: relocation, remote/hybrid policy, and <strong>bond or service agreements</strong> (read carefully: clawbacks, minimum tenure, training cost recovery).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-questions-to-ask-them-ask-two-or-three\">Questions to ask them (ask two or three)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>What does success look like in the first six months?</li>\n<li>What does the data stack and team structure look like?</li>\n<li>How are models deployed and monitored?</li>\n<li>How do data scientists work with product and engineering?</li>\n<li>What are the biggest data challenges right now?</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m15-hr~1","q":"The recruiter asks for your current CTC and expected CTC. How do you respond?","a":"<p>Give your current CTC truthfully if asked directly (it is often verified) with the breakup (fixed vs variable), then steer to the role: &quot;For this role I&#39;m expecting ₹X to ₹Y based on market research and the scope; I&#39;m flexible on the structure and more interested in the overall opportunity. What is the band you have in mind?&quot; Keep it calm and collaborative, and never invent figures.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m15-hr~ca0_1","q":"How do you answer &quot;What are your salary expectations?&quot; early on?","a":"<p>Say you would like to learn the range for the role; if pressed, give a researched market range for the role and level, and say you are flexible for the right fit.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m15-hr~ca0_2","q":"What should a &quot;why this company&quot; answer include?","a":"<p>Specific product or data problems that interest you, a link to your skills, and evidence you researched.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m15-hr~ca0_3","q":"Besides base pay, what else do you compare in offers?","a":"<p>Bonus, equity, benefits, growth, team and learning opportunities, and flexibility.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m15-hr~ta1_1","q":"<p>A recruiter asks for your expected salary in the first call. You have researched that the range for the role is 12 to 16 lakh. What do you say?</p>\n","a":"<p>For example: &quot;I&#39;d like to understand the role and its scope first. Could you share the budgeted range?&quot; If pressed: &quot;Based on my research for this role and level in this city, I&#39;m looking at the 14 to 16 lakh range for fixed pay, and I&#39;m flexible depending on the overall package and the learning the role offers.&quot; This anchors with research, stays in the upper part of the market range, and leaves room to negotiate.</p>\n"}]},"m15-interview-day":{"id":"m15-interview-day","slug":"interview-day-tips","m":15,"title":"Interview-day tips","min":12,"type":"revise","priority":"High","prereq":[],"tags":["interview","tips"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Practical habits for the day of the interview: how to think aloud, handle being stuck, test your work and stay calm and honest.","keyTerms":["think aloud","clarify","edge cases","honesty","partial progress","small examples"],"interviewQs":["What do you do when you are stuck in an interview?","How do you handle a question you don't know?"],"mistakes":["Silence when stuck","Coding before clarifying","Bluffing instead of saying what you do know"],"practice":"Do one mock interview where a friend interrupts you with \"what if the input is empty?\" and \"what is the complexity?\" and notice how you respond.","related":["m1-practice","m2-practice","m15-hr"],"points":"- Think aloud, clarify requirements before coding, state assumptions.\n- For SQL/coding: start simple, test with a small example and edge cases (NULLs, ties, empty input).\n- If stuck: say what you know, propose an approach, ask a targeted question. Silence hurts more than partial progress.\n- Be honest about what you don't know; show how you would find out."},"intuitive":"<p>On the day, how you think matters as much as what you know. Say your thoughts aloud, ask questions before jumping in, start with a simple version, and test it on a tiny example including awkward cases. If you are stuck, say what you do know, propose an approach, and ask a targeted question: silence hurts more than a partial answer. If you genuinely do not know something, say so and explain how you would find out.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Interviewers decide from your reasoning, not just your final answer.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Clarify, think aloud, start simple, test, and be honest.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;On interview day, preparation is mostly logistics and mindset. Before: test the video link, camera, mic and a quiet space; have water, a notepad and the job description; review my project stories. During: listen fully, clarify the question, think aloud, structure the answer, and check in. If stuck, state what I know and reason from basics; silence while thinking is fine if I say so. In coding, I talk through the approach before typing and test with examples. After: send a short thank-you note and write down what was asked while it is fresh. The catch: nerves are normal, so I slow down and breathe, since a calm, structured answer scores higher than a rushed correct one.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"The day, hour by hour","html":"<ol>\n<li><strong>The day before:</strong> confirm the time zone and the link, test camera, microphone and screen sharing, and charge the laptop. Reread your resume, project notes and the job description. Stop studying by early evening and sleep properly.</li>\n<li><strong>One hour before:</strong> a light meal and water nearby; a pen and paper; your three questions to ask. Close notifications.</li>\n<li><strong>The first five minutes:</strong> smile, give your 90-second introduction, and listen closely to how the interviewer describes the round.</li>\n<li><strong>For each question:</strong> restate it, ask one clarifying question, think aloud with a structure, check in (&quot;does that answer what you were asking?&quot;).</li>\n<li><strong>If you go blank:</strong> say &quot;Let me think about this for a moment&quot;, write down what you know, and solve a smaller version aloud. Silence for 20 seconds with a visible plan is fine; panic is not.</li>\n<li><strong>The last five minutes:</strong> ask your questions about the team&#39;s problems and how success is measured.</li>\n<li><strong>Within an hour of finishing:</strong> write down every question you were asked and how you did; send a two-line thank-you note.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Most interview-day failures are avoidable: logistics, nerves, and not using the last five minutes. A routine handles all three.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-checklist","text":"The checklist","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li>Think aloud, clarify requirements before coding, state assumptions.</li>\n<li>For SQL/coding: start simple, test with a small example and edge cases (NULLs, ties, empty input).</li>\n<li>If stuck: say what you know, propose an approach, ask a targeted question. Silence hurts more than partial progress.</li>\n<li>Be honest about what you don&#39;t know; show how you would find out.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-checklist\">The checklist</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Clarify requirements</strong> and state assumptions before coding or answering.</li>\n<li><strong>Think aloud</strong>: name the approach and its trade-offs as you go.</li>\n<li><strong>Start simple</strong> (a brute-force or baseline), then improve.</li>\n<li><strong>Test</strong> on a small example and edge cases: NULLs, ties, empty input, duplicates.</li>\n<li><strong>If stuck:</strong> say what you know, propose an approach, ask a targeted question (&quot;Can I assume the array is sorted?&quot;).</li>\n<li><strong>Be honest</strong> about gaps: &quot;I haven&#39;t used that in production; here&#39;s how I&#39;d reason about it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Manage time</strong>: if a question is taking too long, say so and offer to move on.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m15-interview-day~1","q":"You are asked about a technique you have never used (say, survival analysis). What do you say?","a":"<p>&quot;I haven&#39;t used it directly, but here&#39;s my understanding: it models time until an event and handles censored observations (customers who haven&#39;t churned yet). I&#39;d use it for churn timing questions, with Kaplan-Meier curves for non-parametric estimates and Cox regression for covariate effects. I&#39;d start with a simple baseline and validate on a time split.&quot; Show what you know, connect to a related concept, and say how you would learn the rest.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m15-interview-day~ca0_1","q":"You blank on a SQL question. What do you do?","a":"<p>Restate the problem, write the output columns and a small example, break it into steps (join, filter, aggregate), and talk through each; progress aloud earns credit.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m15-interview-day~ca0_2","q":"What should you do in the first minute of a case question?","a":"<p>Clarify the goal and context, restate the problem, and outline your approach before diving in.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m15-interview-day~ca0_3","q":"What do you do after each round?","a":"<p>Note the questions and your weak spots, and send a brief thank-you message.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m15-interview-day~ta1_1","q":"<p>Halfway through a SQL question you realise your join has been wrong for five minutes. What exactly do you say and do?</p>\n","a":"<p>Say it plainly: &quot;I&#39;ve just noticed my join multiplies rows, because order_items has several rows per order. Let me fix that by aggregating the items first.&quot; Then fix it, and test the corrected query on two or three rows aloud. Catching and fixing your own bug is a positive signal; interviewers watch how you debug, not just whether the first draft was perfect.</p>\n"}]}});